                <pb facs="0828" n="724" sameAs="frus:frus1932v03/pg724"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.91/5109: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China (Johnson) to the Acting
                        Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-04-26T12:00:00+08:00">April 26,
                        1932—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received April 26—2:50 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>My April 23, 2 p.m. High Commission heard from British Minister who is returning
                    from Nanking today that Chinese Government is prepared to accept suggested
                    amendment to paragraph 11 of new draft resolution. That he has so informed his
                    Secretary for Foreign Affairs at Geneva and has told him that Wang Ching-wei and
                    the Chinese Minister for Foreign Affairs have made it plain that this represents
                    China’s last word and limit of her concessions.</p>
                <p>Not repeated to Geneva.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
